# Design: Device-side --exclude globs for discovery (T147)

## Problem
Atuin's failure classes (guardian-internal sessions polluting a store
with no way out, no per-machine opt-out) must not recur here. Operators
need a device-side, per-agent capture control in front of registration.

## Approach
Operator decision 2026-11-19: one mechanism (a single repeatable
--exclude flag on ingest, join, and setup), one enforcement point
(discoverSessionFiles in cmd/ohya/ingest.go, shared by ingest, join, the
uploader, and the backlog report), visible accounting (per-agent skip
counts in the pass summary, ingest --once JSON, and ohya status via the
discovery_excluded{agent} gauge).

## Key Trade-offs
- One shared flag, not per-agent flag names: globs are absolute paths,
  so a glob is naturally scoped by the root it names; accounting stays
  per agent because each agent's walk filters its own roots.
- Stdlib path.Match only (no **, no regex): a deep tree needs a glob per
  directory level; the operator chose simplicity over expressive power.
- Missing-source reports recognize excluded paths by set membership
  against the pass's excluded paths (both path forms stored, since watch
  state keys resolve parent symlinks), not by re-matching globs — exact
  and avoids a second matcher call site.
- watch_sources{state=missing} still counts an excluded source; the
  excluded gauge reconciles. Accepted cosmetic limit.
- An exclusion is going-forward only: stored sessions stay; removal is
  ohya purge's job (ADR 0013).

## Gotchas
- Set("") must clear the list: setup saves Flags["exclude"] = "" when no
  glob is configured, and every setup rerun replays saved flags through
  fs.Set — an error on empty broke both setup e2e suites. Empty-means-
  none matches the --<agent>-root '' convention and doubles as the way
  to clear saved excludes.
- Flag-time validation via filepath.Match(glob, "") misses malformed
  post-star chunks; discovery surfaces those loudly at runtime.

